# NVIDIA's NVFP4 pretraining recipe ("Pretraining Large Language Models with NVFP4",
# 2025; Transformer Engine's NVFP4BlockScaling): E2M1 values with an E4M3 scale per 16
# and a float32 scale per tensor; W in 16 x 16 tiles, so that y and dx see the same W;
# gradients rounded stochastically; and a random Hadamard transform of 16 on what dW
# multiplies. Their 12B hybrid kept its first 2 and last 8 layers in bfloat16, each a
# residual branch here, so a Llama-style block is two. Sweep how many:
#
#   use nvidia_nvfp4
#   precision.last = 4 | 8
#
# Taking fprop to bfloat16 for the last 18% of the tokens cut their loss gap from 1.5%
# to 0.5%: precision.switch = switch(at=0.82, passes=[dgrad, wgrad]).
#
# Left out: NVFP4 routed experts, which stay in bfloat16. bux quantizes NVFP4 in XLA,
# which makes it slower than bfloat16: the recipe is for its numerics.
precision = precision(compute=bfloat16, matmuls=nvfp4, first=2, last=8)
